think Standfast first -- swept crux/REDS.md: 12 rows, every one Released, Closed, or custody-gated errata (#75). No open agent-closable red. Clear to take new constructive work.
think Crux-first pick: the NEXT list named a longest-path EDGE-LIST read naming the bridges the diameter walks (the other agent-doable options were a lasso per-cluster read or a codec read; the Microkernel study and real-photo top-hat both wait on a fetch/license gate, not crossable here). The edge-list read is the tightest lift over the freshest green rung -- cluster_tree_diameter gave the diameter WEIGHT and its two POLES yet never named which bridges the longest walk crosses.
think Built image/cluster_diameter_path.rye: diameter_path reconstructs the ordered walk pole to pole via a parent-recording breadth-first walk (bounded, no recursion), naming the clusters visited in order and the bridge edges crossed; diameter_path_hops the edge count distinct from the weight; is_on_diameter_path the crossed-pair test. Composes over cluster_tree_diameter (diameter_endpoints, tree_diameter cross-checked, never re-derived), cluster_minimum_spanning_tree (build_mst, is_bridge, edge_count), cluster_graph (the matrix a level below).
think The decisive crux in two scenes mirroring the diameter sibling: the three-cluster star where the walk crosses BOTH bridges (the path IS the tree, weights sum to 5 = the diameter), and the four-cluster star where the walk crosses two of three bridges (the third arm 1-3 branches off, is_on_diameter_path(1,3) FALSE for an edge is_bridge(1,3) TRUE) -- the path a STRICT SUBSET the diameter's single number could never resolve.
think Fixed one authoring slip before build: a lone-cluster is_on_diameter_path(1,1) test would hit SameCluster not FALSE, so I dropped that block -- the EmptyTree refusals already carry the lone case. A clarity fix, not an algorithm red, none booked.
